% c418: 205-206 and 207-208 cellos' B and violas' F sharp/B pedal slurred in pairs with louré dashes (dashes so the slur on the repeated note does not read as a tie; the repeated note re-articulated inside one bow; Adler PDF 42-43, Ex. II-30), so the pp< into the trio's return at 207 is one up-bow (was a down-bow), and 209 starts down-bow as before; Adler PDF 38, 39-40.
% c480 (A&S Unit 28, PDF 483: the Neapolitan 6th at climactic moments 'even in places where diatonic II6 would not be incorrect'; PDF 494-495, Ex. 28-19, Schubert D. 959/iii,
%   a 3/4 Allegro vivace scherzo: the Neapolitan 6th of C sharp minor is IV6 of A major): 199 is now the Neapolitan 6th of C sharp minor, D/F sharp (was ii half-dim 6/5,
%   D sharp-F sharp-A-C sharp), at the trio's forte peak, before V (200) and the A major of 201, which it prepares as IV6. T's bar-3 note D sharp -> D in Cl 1, Vln II, Vla (this statement only);
%   Cl 2 D4 -> B sharp 3 at 200 (the diminished third flat 2 - sharp 7, Ex. 28-2a); Hn 2 D4 -> D sharp 4 inner (Ex. 28-3d: possible with flat 2 doubled); bass F sharp doubled in Bsn 1 (PDF 484).
% TRIO, middle part, bars 185-208 (c003: 24 bars; v03 bars 189-196 cut).
% c085 (c355: now on Vln II, see tBVlnTwo): 193 Vla "unis." after the div. thirds of 169-184 (Adler PDF 30). 192 and 208 Cb rest: 5 beats for pizz.->arco at 193 and 209 (Adler PDF 54; harp F sharp 1 at 192, Vc B2 at 208 keep the bass).
% c035: 185 Ob 1 prints "1."; 193-196 Bn 1 solo (\partCombineSoloI: one voice with "1." instead of Bn 2's bar rests; likewise 205-208) (no label was reprinted after the shared rest).
% c022: 200 Ob 2 D sharp, Cl 1 B sharp: no fifths/octaves from iv to V; 203 viola D sharp: no fifths E-A/F sharp-B with the bass (A-S PDF 189-190).
% 185-192: call and response: Ob 1 in C sharp minor (185); the cellos answer alone (c427; the cor anglais doubled them at the unison until v427) a step lower, in B major (189).
%
% 193-200: the strings together, crescendo to the trio's forte: the oboe's call in C sharp minor in Vln I and II, ending on G sharp (V of C sharp).
% 201-208: A major, then A minor (mixture, iv), to the dominant pedal B; the motto tail B-A sharp-A in Fl 2 and Hn 1 (205-208; c036, c050, c324: Hn 1 for Cl 2), so Fl 1 and Cl 1 rest before T at 209; the 7th A (V7) falls to G sharp at 209. (c193: header brought up to date.)

% c042: 192-193 Cl 1 takes the harp's A sharp 4 (pp) and moves to A itself, so F sharp major -> F sharp minor is a chromatic step in one voice (A-S PDF 394, Ex. 23-12b).
% c032: 196-197 V7-vi: the 7th A only in Cl 1 (violas B, not A), Cl 1 A -> G# and Cl 2 D# -> E (the clarinet parts 197-200 exchanged an octave), so the 7th falls
%   and the leading tone rises into the deceptive C sharp minor (A-S PDF 76, 202).
% c033: 207-208 crescendo in the B-pedal parts, the timpani roll and the motto-tail line (Fl 1, Cl 1), so T's forte return at 209 is reached, not dropped in
%   after pp (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, amplification: the second group enters soft and crescendos faster; PDF 130, crescendo strings - wood - brass).
% c034: 197-200 Fl 1 doubles Vln I's E6 call at the trio's forte (Adler II PDF 151-152: the flute alone carries the top octave; Mozart 40/iv, Beethoven 9/i).
% c047: 197 Hn 1-2 mf, a degree under the woodwinds' f: Hn 1 doubles Cl 1's T at the unison and would cover it (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: in forte the horns
%   overwhelm the reeds; PDF 112: mark the woodwind one degree louder than the brass).
% c039: 197-200 no A4 against a held G sharp 4 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 132-133: a florid line and its harmonic basis in one register clash; keep them an octave apart):
%   Hn 1 moves with T (G sharp-A, B, A tied over as a 9-8 suspension into 200), Ob 2 E4 at 197, Ob 1 and Vln II end the call A4-G sharp 4 with T.
% c037: 197-200 Cl 1 and the violas play T's first phrase at its own pitch (G sharp-A-B-E, D sharp-C sharp-B, A-G sharp) against the oboe's call
%   in Fl 1, Ob 1 and the violins, instead of holding G sharp and C sharp: the trio's two ideas combined at its forte, over C sharp minor - F sharp minor - G sharp
%   (Adler II PDF 230-231: Schoenberg's countertheme of the bar's pitches in its own rhythm; PDF 203: layers of linear activity sounding together).
% c061: 195-196 Vln I, Vln II (and Bsn 1) tie G sharp over the barline into the V7 (bass B, Cl 1 A) and resolve to F sharp on beat 2: the 19th-century
%   7/6-5 suspension over V7 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 353-354 sec. 15, Ex. 21-15e-f, Josef Strauss), so the string build no longer repeats T's
%   rhythm 4.-8-4 | 2. (c021) and the crescendo into 197 peaks on a dissonance (PDF 348: suspensions bring a line into prominence).
% c091: 185, 189, 197 the call's first bar is a real five-note turn (main tone, upper neighbour, main tone, lower neighbour, main tone: E-F#-E-D#-E;
%   answer D#-E-D#-C#-D#) instead of E-D#-C#-D#-E, a lower passing excursion that has no upper neighbour and anticipates the C# of the next bar
%   (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 338-339, Ex. 20-21b Mahler: the nineteenth-century turn; PDF 335: the neighbour figure treated as a motive).
% c117: 198-200 the call (Fl 1-2, Vln I, Ob 1) moves where the trio tune holds (198 C sharp crotchet, G sharp minim; 199 A minim, B-C sharp quavers), instead of
%   doubling T's minim-crotchet and dotted rhythm in contrary motion; Ob 1 leaves the call's lower octave after the turn (197) and joins the top line at C sharp 6,
%   so it no longer crosses Cl 1/Vln II/Vla/Hn 1 at G sharp 4-E5 and meets them in unison on A4-G sharp 4 at 200 (Adler II PDF 177, 179: combined elements need
%   distinct rhythms and their own colour and register; PDF 172: oboe doubling at pitch gives the violins a sharper edge). T's recorded statement is unchanged.
% c167: 197-200 Fl 1 and Ob 1 mf (was f) on Vln I's forte call: one woodwind weighs about as much as sixteen violins, so the winds are marked a level under the violins they double (Sebesky PDF 164); the tune below (Vln II, Vla, Cl 1, Hn 1) is no longer pressed down by two winds at f in the sixth octave.
% c387: 205-208 Fl 1 joins Fl 2 a2 on the octave above Hn 1's motto-tail preview (G sharp-A-B-A sharp-A), and both flutes resolve to G sharp 5 at 209 (tC):
%   one flute in its weak middle octave (G sharp 5-B5) over a single horn in its strong middle register was the only place where a horn line is doubled at the octave by one wind;
%   Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 75 (B. wind and brass in octaves): "If a single horn is used, the upper part is allotted to 2 clarinets, 2 oboes, or 2 flutes" (2 Fl. over 1 Horn), and PDF 75 (A): a wind added to a brass
%   instrument gives a finer legato. c036's reason for giving the bars to Fl 2 alone (Fl 1 had 205-224 without rest) no longer holds: Fl 1 now rests 201-204 and 210-216 and enters at 217.
tBFluteOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'''4(\mp^\markup \bold "1." fis'''8 e'''8 dis'''8 e'''8 | cis'''4 gis''2) | a''2( b''8 cis'''8) | gis''2.\> | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis''2(\p^\markup \italic "espress." a''4 | b''2.~ | b''2\< ais''4 | a''2.) | }  % c036: 205-208 go to Fl 2, so that Fl 1 enters fresh with T at 209 (it had 20 bars, 205-224, with no rest; Adler PDF 188, Brahms 1/iv: the second flute takes over so the first can breathe; PDF 192: multiple flutes relieve one another)
% c084: 197-200 Fl 2 doubles Fl 1's call a2 at E6 (was C sharp 5-B sharp 4 held f, the flute's weak low register inside oboes, clarinets, horns and violas;
%   Casanelles PDF 275: the flute's weak fundamental in its low register; PDF 287: sounds in one band mask each other). B sharp stays in Cl 2 and Bsn 1.
% c119: 197-200 Fl 2 rests (c084's a2 removed; its objection to Fl 2's low C sharp 5-B sharp 4 still holds, so no low note returns): with Ob 1 joining the top line at 198 (c117),
%   Fl 1-2 and Ob 1 were three winds in unison on Vln I alone, over the tune in Vla, Vln II, Cl 1 and Hn 1 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76: several winds in unison with one string group overpower it, as c111 applied at 41-43).
%   Now Fl 1 + Ob 1 + Vln I: the oboe's call keeps its colour above the flute (PDF 65, Ex. 52: Fl + Ob, the oboe leads in the upper register), and at 197 Fl 1/Ob 1 stand in the natural octave (PDF 67).
% c158: 205-208 the motto tail anticipated (G sharp-A-B-A sharp-A, Fl 2 and Cl 2 in octaves) is the primary line: both p espress. (Fl 2 was pp, under its own octave), the held background (Vla F sharp, Vc B, Cb pizz.) pp from 205 (Sebesky PDF 13-14 focus; PDF 210, Ex. 277: a long-note lead line needs character; as c058 at 357).
% c414: 205-208 Fl 2 rests; Fl 1 alone takes the octave over Hn 1's motto-tail preview (c387's a2 removed). Adler I PDF 249-250: two like winds in unison on an ordinary solo
%   passage thicken and muddy the tone, alter the solo timbre and prohibit the slight rubato one player adds; for winds a2 the octave (8-foot + 4-foot) is the norm. Here the octave is
%   already there (Hn 1 8-foot, flute 4-foot); a second flute only added a second 4-foot rank in unison. A MIDI scan of v414 found this the movement's only soft like-wind unison line
%   (all others are ff tuttis or builds: 37-44, 101-120, 273-276, 305-320, 334-339). c387's balance reason (Rimsky PDF 75, 2 Fl over 1 Hn) answered: the flute here is the 4-foot colour of the
%   horn's line, not an equal octave (Adler PDF 250, organ registration), and the horn is the motto's one colour (Adler PDF 261, c324). Fl 2 also rests at 209 (tC).
tBFluteTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c023: 206-209 Fl 1 and Cl 1 hold B, then A sharp (207, beat 3) and A (208, the V7's 7th) to G sharp at 209: the motto's tail B-A sharp-A-G sharp,
%   at the pitch of Hn 1's close (coda), leads into T's return (A&S PDF 600-602: Mozart K. 542 quotes and enlarges the same B-A sharp-A-G sharp).
% c021: the calls at 185, 189 and 197 open with a quaver turn, so the trio's middle part does not repeat T's rhythm (2-4 | 2-4 | 4.-8-4 | 2.) a fifth to eighth time
% c135: "1." at 197 (Ob 1 alone after eight bars rest, as Fl 1; MOLA PDF 4, Ex. 6)
% c278: 187-188 and 191-192 the calls swell into the rising A-B-C sharp (G sharp-A-B) and fall away on the held last note (hidden mp), so Ob 1 clears the cor anglais' answer and the answer clears the strings at 193 (Adler PDF 205-206: Berlioz, Wagner and Sibelius cor anglais solos shape their held notes with < >; PDF 227 Tchaikovsky 6 bassoon pp< > p< >).
% c279: 197 no hand-written "1." on Ob 1: since c278's hairpins at 187-189 the part-combiner prints its own solo label here, and the two stacked "1." marks read as a misprint (as c225 at 193; Adler II PDF 217, 224: notate every detail exactly; MOLA PDF 8)
tBOboeOne = { e''4(\p^\markup { \bold "1." \italic "espress." } fis''8 e''8 dis''8 e''8 | cis''2 gis'4) | a'4.(\< b'8 cis''4) | gis'2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e''4(\mp fis''8 e''8 dis''8 e''8 | cis'''4 gis''2) | a''2( b''8 cis'''8) | gis''2.\> | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c094: 197-200 Ob 2 rests (it held E4-G sharp 4-F sharp 4-D sharp 4 in its low register under Cl 1's T, in unison with Hn 2, and in 2nds, 7ths, 4ths and 5ths with Ob 1's call;
%   Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rules 2-4: normal order of register, matching registers, concords to two instruments of one colour, 'specially' the oboe). The notes stay in Hn 2 and Cl 2.
tBOboeTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c427: 189-192 the cor anglais rests; the cellos answer Ob 1's call alone, on the A string (Adler II PDF 257-258, Ex. XVII-1: the answer in a radically different colour, Beethoven's answering strings even pizzicato 'to avoid duplication of the solo sound'; PDF 264, 267: no instrument of the solo's own colour beside it). The cor anglais is an oboe: the answer kept half the call's reed colour.
tBCorAnglais = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c129: 193 Cl 1 A4 (2 beats) then C sharp 5 tied into 194 (was A4 held through 193): the violins' passing G sharp 4 on beat 3 no longer rubs against a held A4
%   in its own register (c039's rule; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 116, 132-133). The chromatic A sharp-A (192-193) is kept.
% c358: 197 "espress." on Cl 1 and Vln II, which play T's head in unison with the violas (marked espress.) at the same f (Sebesky Ex. 281, PDF 214-215; Ex. 2A, PDF 18).
% c477: 194 and 196 Cl 1 answers the violas' turn (193 A-B-A-G sharp-A, 195 B-C sharp-B-A-B) a bar later, in the call's rhythm (crotchet, four quavers): 194 C sharp 5-D sharp-C sharp-B-C sharp
%   (was C sharp 5 held), 196 B4-C sharp-B-A-B, the violas' 195 turn an octave up (was A4 held; the 7th A sounds on beat 3 with Vln II and Hn 1 and falls to G sharp at 197).
%   Strings and winds pass the call's turn bar by bar into the forte at 197 (Adler PDF 253-254, Eroica 'throwing the ball around'; PDF 271-272, Mozart 38/iii: wind imitation a bar apart, 'especially rhythmical').
tBClOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | ais'2.(\pp | a'2\p\< cis''4~) | cis''4( dis''8 cis''8 b'8 cis''8 | b'2.) | b'4( cis''8 b'8 a'8 b'8) | gis'2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4 | d''4. \once \override NoteHead.extra-spacing-width = #'(-2.6 . 0) fis'8 a'4\<~ | a'4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> gis'2) | \partCombineSoloII R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic }  % c036: 205-208 to Cl 2 (the D sharp stays in Hn 3), so Cl 1 rests 201-208 before T at 209 (Adler PDF 188, 192)
