% c135: "1." at 193 (Bsn 1 alone after 48 bars rest; the automatic "1." first appears at 201)
% c225: no hand-written "1." at 193: \partCombineSoloI already prints the solo label, and the two stacked "1." marks read as a misprint (MOLA PDF 8: proofread the vertical lines; Ex. 6 names the player once).
% c272: 197 Bsn 1 E3 (was G sharp 3): F sharp 3-G sharp 3 over the bass B-C sharp made fifths into the deceptive cadence V7-vi; the third of vi is doubled instead (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 83, Ex. 5-19)
tBBsnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI a2.\p\< | a2. | gis2.~ | gis4( fis4) r4 | \partCombineAutomatic e2.\f | gis2. | fis2. | dis'2.\> | cis'2.\p | c'2. | b2.\> | a2. | \partCombineSoloI fis2.\pp | fis2. | \partCombineAutomatic fis2.\pp\< | fis2 r4 | }  % c066: Bsn 1 breathes before T's return at 209 (held notes 197-216 otherwise, about 16 s; Adler PDF 176, 292)
% c503 (A-S Unit 18, PDF 293-294 Ex. 18-6f-j ascending 6/3 chords; PDF 290-291 6/3s as transition, only the first and last articulated; Unit 28 PDF 485: I6 before the
%   Neapolitan 6th allows a stepwise ascent in the bass): 198 Bsn 2, Vc, Cb E (was C sharp): i - i6 - bII6 - V, two 6/3 chords in a rising bass C sharp-E-F sharp-G sharp-A
%   from the forte call into the A major of 201; Hn 2 E4-C sharp 4 at 198 (was E4 held) so it does not move E-F sharp in octaves with the bass.
tBBsnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic cis2.\f | e,2. | fis,2. | gis,2.\> | a,2.\p | a,2. | b,2.\> | b,2. | \partCombineSoloI R2.\! | R2. | \partCombineAutomatic b,2.\pp\< | b,2 r4 | }  % c075: Bsn 2 shares Bsn 1's diminuendo at 203-204 (the shared staff prints one hairpin; Bsn 2's own part lacked it; MOLA PDF 8)
tBCbsn = { R2.*22 | b,,2.\pp\< | b,,2 r4 | }  % c179: Bsn 2 and Cbsn join the B pedal at 207 pp< so the crescendo into T's return at 209 is made by entries, not hairpins alone (Adler II PDF 201-202, Beethoven 6 'Bassi pp cresc.'; PDF 213 Bizet's orchestrated crescendo); Bsn 1 now crescendos with the violas it doubles.
% c088: 197-200 Hn 1-2 slurred like the Cl 1/violas phrase they join; 201-208 Hn 3-4 portato slurs (Adler I PDF 309: one slur, one breath; soft tonguing).
% c172: 196 Hn 1 doubles Vln II, G sharp 4-F sharp 4-A4 (was F sharp 4 for the whole bar: the 6-5's tone of resolution sounded on the downbeat a tone under Vln II's suspended G sharp 4, A-S Unit 27 PDF 469, PDF 474 Ex. 27-16b; and F sharp 4-G sharp 4 over the bass B-C sharp at 196-197 made the fifths c112 removed from Vln II). 205-208 violas F sharp 3 (was F sharp 4 a tone under Cl 2's G sharp 4 at 205): the 6th over V stands clear of the 5th (A-S PDF 469-470 Ex. 27-10 wide spacing; PDF 474-475 Ex. 27-18).
% c324: 205-208 the motto tail anticipated (G sharp-A-B-A sharp-A) passes from Cl 2 to Hn 1, under Fl 2's octave (Adler I PDF 261-262: one colour for one recurring
%   gesture clarifies the form; PDF 250: octave doubling, not a unison of two colours, PDF 257). Hn 1 has T's head G sharp-A-B-A-G sharp at 197-200 and the motto
%   tail B4-A sharp 4-A4-G sharp 4 at 357-360 at the same pitch; now the horn turns T's head into the tail here as well. Hn 1 resolves A4 -> G sharp 4 at 209 (tC).
% c482: 198 Hn 1 b'2 cis''4 (was b'2.): since c480's Neapolitan 6th at 199 the violas and Cl 2 move E4 -> D4 (T's lower octave) under Hn 1's B4 -> A4, parallel perfect 5ths
%   into the N6 at the trio's forte peak (in v480 the violas' D sharp 4 made P5 -> d5). Hn 1 steps up to C sharp 5 (root of C sharp minor) on beat 3 and falls a 3rd to A4,
%   which it still holds as the 9-8 into 200 (c039): M6 -> P5, no parallels (A-S PDF 267: in repeated patterns 5ths and octaves become a problem and a voice-leading tone
%   prevents them; Unit 5 PDF 83). Only Hn 1's beat 3 of 198 changes; T, the call and every other part are as before.
tBHnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis'2.~(\pp\< | gis'4 fis'4 a'4) | gis'2(\mf a'4 | b'2 cis''4 | a'2.~ | a'4\> gis'2) | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI gis'2(\p^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2.~ | b'2\< ais'4 | a'2.\>) | \partCombineAutomatic }
% c327: 199 Hn 2 F sharp 4 for one beat, then D sharp 4 (was F sharp 4 held): c321's new T bar 3 (D sharp 5-F sharp 4-B4) dropped onto Hn 2's held F sharp 4, so the falling sixth's goal
%   was already sounding in the horn and merged with it (Adler II PDF 273: instruments blend in sympathetic registers; PDF 274: keep the accompaniment registrally apart from the solo).
%   Hn 2 now moves on beat 2 while the tune holds D sharp 5 (PDF 259-260: rhythmic independence) and takes the D sharp 4 it held at 200 a bar early (root of the half-diminished 6/5, c322);
%   it reaches the octave by oblique motion, not from E4 (no octaves with the tune's E5-D sharp 5 at 198-199).
tBHnTwo = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'2.(\pp\< | dis'2.) | e'2.--(\mf | e'2-- cis'4-- | fis'4-- d'2-- | dis'2.--\>) | R2.\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBHnThree = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | e'2.--(\pp | e'2.-- | dis'2.--\> | dis'2--) r4\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }  % c390: Hn 3-4 rest 205-208 under Hn 1's motto-tail preview (their D sharp 4 goes to Cl 2): Mozart never uses the orchestral horns while the solo horn plays (Adler II PDF 264, Ex. XVII-9), so all three motto quotations (3-6, 205-208, 357-360) are one horn among silent horns, as movement I's call (I 13-14)
% c147: 201-204 Hn 4 holds A3, then F sharp 3 (was C sharp 4-C4-B3-B3 in unison with Bsn 1): the soft horn-bassoon chord had three pitches for six players (C sharp 4 x3, E4 x3 with Cl 2 and violas)
%   over an empty tenor A2-C sharp 4, and no F sharp at 203. Now four real parts: Bn 2 bass, Hn 4 the stationary root/fifth, Bsn 1 the moving chromatic line C sharp-C-B-A (Vln I's flat 6 to 5 still an octave lower, c070),
%   Hn 3 the held E-D sharp (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108: two bassoons and two horns in four-part harmony for soft passages; stationary parts to the brass, moving parts to the woodwind, discords to the bassoons).
%   c112's doubled third of VI at 201 (Cl 2, Bsn 1) is kept.
tBHnFour = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | a2.--(\pp | a2.-- | fis2.--\> | fis2--) r4\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }  % c390: rests 205-208 (see tBHnThree); c374's F sharp 3 at 207-208 stays in Bsn 1, the B3 in the violas  % c374: 207-208 Hn 4 F sharp 3 (with Bsn 1), violas take the B3: Hn 1's tail A sharp 4-A4 no longer makes a major/minor seventh against a B3 in the same horn colour (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102-103: discords of the seventh or second to instruments of different colour; PDF 106: 2 horns + bassoons in unison)
% c248: 207-209 the B roll crescendos through 208 into the E stroke f at 209 (was tied into a crotchet mf on 208's downbeat and silent for beats 2-3):
%   the peak fell on the fourth, weak bar of the group 205-208 and the drum stopped a bar before T's forte return, while violas, cellos and Hn 3-4 crescendo through 208.
%   The goal is the hyper-downbeat 209, where the phrase begins and the tonic arrives (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 3, PDF 53-54: bars 1 and 3 strong, the group ends on its tonal goal;
%   PDF 51: the beginning of a note is heard as stronger than its continuation, so the roll's release stroke stood as an accent on a weak bar). As the score's other rolls into an arrival on another drum (130/131, 286/287, 332/333).
% c416: G♯ at 200 damped (staccato, as at 56): undamped it rang f into the A-major chord at 201, a semitone under the cellos' and bassoon's A2 (Adler II PDF 58 out-of-harmony timpani note; PDF 80 show how long a note may ring)
