% c027: 189-192 harp chords an octave up (B4-F sharp 5) above the cellos' and cor anglais' answer (F sharp 3-D sharp 4), which they covered and crossed (Adler II PDF 257, 274)
% c116: 193 étouffez: the A sharp 4-C sharp 5-F sharp 5 chord of 192 is damped when A natural enters (Cl 1 A4, violas and Bsn 1 A3), so the cross relation c042 removed is not re-sounded by ringing strings (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46)
% c170 (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 23, PDF 387 and 393-395): 188 the G sharp major chord (III sharp of E, V of C sharp minor; enharmonically movement II's A flat major) is spelled with its third B sharp, not C natural: C natural is the trio's flat 6 (202, 235, 359), so the harp no longer shows a flat 6 where the harmony has sharp 5. Pedals: B sharp for 188, B natural for 189, C stays sharp (190 now marks only A sharp).
% c192: 191-192 IV-V of B: the harp's upper voices fall G sharp 4-F sharp 4, B4-A sharp 4, E5-C sharp 5 against the bass rising E2-F sharp 2 (were all three rising with the bass leaping down a seventh,
%   octaves and fifths E-F sharp / B-C sharp by contrary motion), as the call does at 187-188 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 136 sec. 3, Ex. 9-3a: IV-V has no common tone, lead the upper voices against the bass; PDF 86: fifths and octaves by contrary motion are avoided).
% c256: "non arpegg." on the first off-beat chord of each accompaniment entry (81, 185, 225): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46, harp chords "must always be broken (arpeggiato); should the composer wish otherwise he should notify it (non arpeggiato)". 153 and 341 already say so; 81 said nothing, and 185 and 225 follow 16 bars rest at a new letter. Chords meant broken keep their arpeggio sign (149, 151, 237, 361).
tBHarpR = { r4 <gis cis' e'>4_\markup \small \italic "non arpegg." <gis cis' e'>4 | r4 <gis cis' e'>4 <gis cis' e'>4 | r4 <a cis' fis'>4 <a cis' fis'>4 | r4_\markup \small "B♯" <gis bis dis'>4 <gis bis dis'>4 | r4_\markup \small "B♮" <b' dis'' fis''>4 <b' dis'' fis''>4 | r4 <b' dis'' fis''>4_\markup \small "A♯" <b' dis'' fis''>4 | r4 <gis' b' e''>4 <gis' b' e''>4 | r4 <fis' ais' cis''>4 << <fis' ais' cis''>4 { s8 s8_\markup \small \italic "étouffez" } >> | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c099: harp pp (was p, c094) under Ob 1's p espress. call and the cor anglais/cello answer (Casanelles PDF 271-272).
% c206: 189 LH rests (was b,,4 r4 r4). The B pedal must go from B sharp (188 b3 chord) back to B natural in the RH rest at 189 b1, and the LH B1 on that same downbeat left no time for it; the basses keep the pizz. B (Adler part 1 PDF 111, 113).
tBHarpL = { cis,4\pp r4 r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | R2. | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | fis,4 r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
tBVlnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | fis''2(\p\< gis''4 | a''2 cis'''4) | b''4.( a''8 gis''4~ | gis''4 fis''2) | e'''4(\mf fis'''8 e'''8 dis'''8 e'''8 | cis'''4 gis''2) | a''2( b''8 cis'''8) | gis''2.\> | a'4--\p r4 r4 | c''4-- r4 r4 | b'4-- r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }  % c070: 203 the top voice's flat 6 (C5, A minor at 202) falls to 5 (B4) over the B pedal in its own register, with Bsn 1 and Hn 4 an octave lower (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 23, PDF 388 and PDF 401 point 3: once introduced, flat 6 moves to 5)
% c107: 197-200 Vln II plays the trio tune's head with the violas (unison G sharp 4-E5) instead of the call's lower octave, which crossed the violas' tune in the same colour
%   and register and met it in rhythm at 199-200 (Adler I PDF 144 rules 3-4: theme and countertheme apart in register and rhythm; PDF 135: background out of the melody's register in like colour).
%   The call keeps Fl 1-2 and Vln I at E6 and Ob 1 at E5.
% s125 (cycle 125): 201-202 Vln II tenutos above: under C sharp 4/E4 they sat where a ledger line goes and the one at 201 touched the p (Gould PDF 55-56)
% c407: "unis." at 193 removed: Vln II is no longer divided at 169-184 (one quaver line, tA.ily).
% c355: 193 "unis." moved from the violas to Vln II. Since c344 Vln II plays the divided off-beat dyads at 169-184 and returns to single notes here;
%   the violas have not been divided since their non div. double stops at 105-120 (Adler I PDF 30: when divisi ends, unis. on the part).
% c358: 197 "espress." on Cl 1 and Vln II, which play T's head in unison with the violas (marked espress.) at the same f (Sebesky Ex. 281, PDF 214-215; Ex. 2A, PDF 18).
% c426: 185 "via sord." on the first rest bar after the muted arpeggios 169-184: Adler II PDF 312 gives "take off mutes" (via sordini) and "without mute" (senza sordino)
%   as separate terms; the first says when to take the mute off (eight bars, about 6 s at 2. = 76), the second (kept at 193) the state at the entry. Movement II uses "via sord." the same way (II 91).
%   Score: zero-size markup so page 7 does not overfill (with full size the score went to 14 pages); part: normal markup above the multi-bar rest.
tBVlnTwo = { R2. -\tag #'score ^\markup \with-dimensions #'(0 . 0) #'(0 . 0) \italic "via sord." -\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "via sord." | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | fis'2(\p\<^\markup \italic "senza sord." gis'4 | a'2 cis''4) | b'4.( a'8 gis'4~ | gis'4 fis'4 a'4) | gis'2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." a'4 | b'2 e''4) | d''4.( fis'8 a'4\<~ | a'4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> gis'2) | cis'4^-\p r4 r4 | e'4^- r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c177: 201-203 violas hold the top line A4-C5-B4 (the flat 6-5 of c070) under Vln I's tenuto crotchets, instead of a fourth E4 (with Cl 2, Hn 3 and Vln II) and a third D sharp 4 (Adler I PDF 257: unison overlapping only gives a stronger pitch; PDF 255: the line must come through the harmony; PDF 270, Copland: the weak line gets the doubling). Bar 204 F sharp 4 unchanged.
% c212: 195 violas' turn B-C sharp-B-A-B (was A sharp): the diatonic repetition of the turn on A at 193 (A-B-A-G sharp-A), as the cellos' answer at 189 takes the
%   whole-step lower neighbour C sharp, not C double sharp (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 265: in a diatonic sequence the melodic intervals change to fit the key). The A sharp 3
%   came right after Vln I/II's A5/A4 quaver and before Cl 1's A4 at 196, two chromatic successions split between voices (A-S PDF 394, Ex. 23-12a).
% c437 (Adler II PDF 229, Schoenberg's Brahms op. 25: where two similar colours at pitch 'would swallow each other up', double at the octave for a richer sound; PDF 239: two loud phrases exchange tessitura, the winds doubled in the lower octave by the second violins; PDF 213: the repeat is fuller): 197-200 the violas play T's head an octave lower (G sharp 3-A3-B3-E4, D sharp 4-F sharp 3-A3 tied | A3-G sharp 3; was at pitch with Vln II and Cl 1), so the trio's forte head sounds in two octaves, the upper at its first pitch (Cl 1 153; Cl 1, Vln II, Hn 1 here) and the lower at the cellos' and violas' pitch of 169-184. The violas step B3-G sharp 3 into 197 (was a leap of a sixth to G sharp 4). Notes and rhythm unchanged.
tBVla = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | a4(\p\< b8 a gis a) | a2. | b4( cis'8 b a b) | b2. | gis2(\f_\markup \italic "espress." a4 | b2 e'4) | d'4.( fis8 a4\<~ | a4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> gis2) | e'4(\p a'2) | c''2. | b'2.\> | fis'2. | fis2.--(\pp | fis2.--) | b2.--(\< | b2.--) | }
% c102: 195 bass B, tied into 196 (was G sharp, I6 between ii7 and V7): ii7 - V6/4 - V7, the cadential 6/4 approached by intermediate harmony; Cl 2 E4 is the 4th held as a common tone and falls to D sharp 4 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 148 rule 3, 152-153, 160).
tBVc = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \clef bass dis'4(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" e'8 dis'8 cis'8 dis'8 | b2 fis4) | gis4.(\< a8 b4) | fis2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | fis,2.\p\< | fis,2. | b,2.~ | b,2. | cis2.\f | e,2. | fis,2. | gis,2.\> | a,2.\p | a,2. | b,2.\> | b,2. | b,2.--(\pp | b,2.--) | b,2.--(\< | b,2.--) | }  % c011: the answer at 189-192 an octave higher, on the A and D strings, in unison with the cor anglais (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 56-57)
% c158: 185 Cb pizz. pp (was p carried from 169): the harp doubles this bass at pp under Ob 1's p solo; the pizz. bass under the trio solos is pp at 153 and 341 (c099, c148; Sebesky PDF 14: the rhythm section is the tertiary level).
% c265: 207 the basses' third pizz. B is marked p (was a hairpin from a single plucked note across the rest at 208): a plucked note cannot swell, so the crescendo into 209 is made by a louder pluck (pp, pp, p) and by the bowed parts (Adler I PDF 37-38, 53-54: crescendo and diminuendo are bow functions; pizz. is plucked; as c179 did for Vln II at 271).
